+# What the wait numbers show since the tablet
+
+**Short version:** waits on the dashboard really did jump when the tablet arrived on 16 March. The dashboard had bugs, and they are now fixed. But the bugs did not cause that jump. They hid part of it.
+
+## The corrected weekly numbers
+
+| week of | visits | seen | no "seen" time (counted as left) | median wait (min) | old dashboard said |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 23 Feb (paper) | 37 | 36 | 1 | 17 | 23 |
+| 2 Mar (paper) | 44 | 42 | 2 | 16.5 | 21 |
+| 9 Mar (paper) | 37 | 36 | 1 | 22 | 27 |
+| 16 Mar (tablet) | 37 | 33 | 4 | 31 | 31 |
+| 23 Mar (tablet) | 43 | 39 | 4 | 33 | 33 |
+| 30 Mar (tablet) | 33 | 29 | 4 | 39 | 39 |
+
+The old dashboard made the paper weeks look slower than they were, and it left out the people with no "seen" time. For the tablet weeks the old and corrected medians agree. So the bugs made the jump look smaller, not bigger. The 9 March week is higher mostly because of one slow day (10 March). Waits weren't creeping up before the switch: across the paper weeks the drift is at most a few minutes. The jump of about 15 minutes happens at the changeover.
+
+## Paper weeks against tablet weeks
+
+- **Typical wait roughly doubled:** 17 minutes on paper, 34 on the tablet.
+- **The quickest quarter of patients** waited up to 11 minutes on paper and up to 23 on the tablet. **The slowest quarter** waited 24 minutes or more on paper and 45 or more on the tablet.
+- On paper, 39 of 114 patients were seen in under 14 minutes. On the tablet, none of 101 were.
+- On paper, nobody waited more than 34 minutes. On the tablet, 48 of 101 did.
+- **No "seen" time (counted as left without being seen):** 4 of 118 on paper, 12 of 113 on the tablet. If nothing had really changed, a gap this big would turn up by luck about 1 time in 25.
+
+## Why I wouldn't compare the two periods directly
+
+Paper and tablet measure different moments. On paper, a person wrote the times down. On the tablet, "signed in" and "seen" are whatever the tablet records when someone taps it. So a doubling could mean patients really wait longer, or it could mean the tablet starts and stops the clock at different moments than the sheet did. The records can't tell these apart.
+
+Walkouts are the better signal, but they aren't clean either. The dashboard counts anyone without a "seen" time as a walkout. On the tablet, that includes anyone whose "seen" nobody pressed.
+
+**One question for you:** on 25 March, 27 March and 2 April there are only morning sign-ins or only afternoon ones. Every other day has sign-ins both before 11 and after 1 pm. Were those half days, or did the tablet miss part of the day?
+
+## Three checks that would settle it
+
+None of these needs anything extra from the front desk.
+
+1. **Call the vendor.** Ask exactly what makes the tablet record "signed in" and "seen". Ask whether it can drop or delay entries.
+2. **Check the tablet's clock** against a phone.
+3. **Time about 10 patients yourself** one morning, from arrival to being called, and note anyone who leaves. If your times match the tablet's, the waits are real.
+
+If the tablet has a "left without being seen" button, ask for it to be turned on. Then a walkout would be something somebody records, not a button nobody pressed.
